#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381201 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "79107609-77e6-55e9-970d-da63c39daf0a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Curve Defined Parametrically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab374465-0f5d-5ef1-ac40-b3e35c2ef8a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=(2x+1)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381203} {:answer/id #uuid "158970c7-5b1d-57f4-9b6a-03b6ca1ae2cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2x(x-5)(2x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381204} {:answer/id #uuid "b65139e2-0c50-5423-b3a0-6c9691d0733a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4x(2x+1)(2x-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381205} {:answer/id #uuid "da9cd34f-c2ab-518b-8132-46abdb181301" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2(2x+1)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381206} {:answer/id #uuid "a2e14081-8b1c-529e-ae73-129b8cac5b07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4x(x+5)(2x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381207}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2e14081-8b1c-529e-ae73-129b8cac5b07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4x(x+5)(2x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381207} :answer-field/id #uuid "e83a50a6-01bf-5730-9930-1af38c0fb461" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381202}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f662300f-f189-53c3-b854-815e04ddab5a" :question/math-academy-id "q-102668" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{t-5}{2},\\,\\,\\,y=({t}^{2}-25)(t-4),\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{t-5}{2}\\,⇒\\,t=2x+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=2x+5$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =((2x+5)^{2}-25)(2x+5-4) \\\\  & =(4{x}^{2}+20x+25-25)(2x+1) \\\\  & =(4{x}^{2}+20x)(2x+1) \\\\  & =4x(x+5)(2x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=4x(x+5)(2x+1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339012 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "78dbecb4-68d4-53b0-b47f-f37ce92da896" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Curve Defined Parametrically With Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e012e206-3735-52c5-a282-bf2808614912" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2x}{\\sqrt{2+{x}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339014} {:answer/id #uuid "856326c3-7667-54fa-a934-161221b007ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339015} {:answer/id #uuid "e800c784-13ae-5601-9c36-93a4a0f3cd63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339016} {:answer/id #uuid "fc573f65-a24e-520c-8eed-2b957f1759f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{4x}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339017} {:answer/id #uuid "d421e68e-e389-5822-acdd-369907b97f6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339018}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e800c784-13ae-5601-9c36-93a4a0f3cd63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339016} :answer-field/id #uuid "48eb6605-a5a6-5deb-9bd1-c52985f6ec88" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339013}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "27bea004-4b63-5df6-b1f8-256ce7d09483" :question/math-academy-id "q-15759" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,0<t<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=x \\\\ y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡t=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡t & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t & =\\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe used here that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t>0$ in the range $0<t<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nNow, using the definition of a tangent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡t=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡t}}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=x$ and $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t=y$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381208 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "78dbecb4-68d4-53b0-b47f-f37ce92da896" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Curve Defined Parametrically With Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19c9a8c6-e009-53b4-b12c-0191de7a91a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2(1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381210} {:answer/id #uuid "f5ab26b6-08da-5425-8654-994a301549cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3(1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381211} {:answer/id #uuid "dc5f842c-bee5-53ec-b816-9d6d014e513f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381212} {:answer/id #uuid "85c9a6ce-7931-5053-8f42-5a2270259829" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381213} {:answer/id #uuid "876b5dc7-1fb2-5f36-929d-970b1e677209" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3(1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381214}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "876b5dc7-1fb2-5f36-929d-970b1e677209" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3(1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381214} :answer-field/id #uuid "a92445cb-46d5-5049-82b6-6f64444df8e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381209}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "615bfb39-f4dc-5e2d-9395-b7631c9499f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-24378" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using double angle formula for cosine and the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =(1-{sin}^{2}⁡t)-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x}{2}$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{3}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{3} & =1-2{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{y}{3} & =1-2(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}) \\\\ \\frac{y}{3} & =1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2} \\\\ y & =3(1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=3(1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307062 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "78dbecb4-68d4-53b0-b47f-f37ce92da896" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Curve Defined Parametrically With Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f94cb9c0-1aef-5920-9e36-292ab6763aa4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307064} {:answer/id #uuid "e997ce2d-a1d4-59a7-b20d-754d3ed14510" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=7(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307065} {:answer/id #uuid "c541779d-bbe7-5c87-8f30-1ed1b67ea24f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{9}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307066} {:answer/id #uuid "e55f241a-779f-5e97-b1b7-f13353144ee3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307067} {:answer/id #uuid "ca950cea-4977-5929-b108-f1f297fada93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307068}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c541779d-bbe7-5c87-8f30-1ed1b67ea24f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{9}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307066} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d956d25-b226-5c60-804c-520274c8dd5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307063}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a1295497-95f0-563c-9b4d-4d497affdde7" :question/math-academy-id "q-24575" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=-\\frac{x}{3} \\\\ y=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using double angle formula for cosine and the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-(1-{cos}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=-\\frac{x}{3}$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{4}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{4} & =2{(-\\frac{x}{3})}^{2}-1 \\\\ \\frac{y}{4} & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{9}-1 \\\\ y & =4(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{9}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=4(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{9}-1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381215 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "79107609-77e6-55e9-970d-da63c39daf0a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Curve Defined Parametrically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b02216e-1888-53d2-a339-64d43470e022" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x^4+6x^2-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381217}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b02216e-1888-53d2-a339-64d43470e022" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x^4+6x^2-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381217} :answer-field/id #uuid "efc94e2d-73ec-5487-af0a-0d200a851b62" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381216}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9c4b3f61-cafc-57f5-8068-37339299eacb" :question/math-academy-id "q-321084" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{\\,t+1\\,},\\,\\,\\,y=t(4-t),\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,x\\ge 1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\sqrt{\\,t+1\\,}\\,⇒\\,t={x}^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t={x}^{2}-1$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =({x}^{2}-1)(4-({x}^{2}-1)) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-1)(5-{x}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=({x}^{2}-1)(5-{x}^{2}).$\n\nFinally, since $t\\ge 0$ and $t={x}^{2}-1,$ we must have ${x}^{2}-1\\ge 0,$ i.e., $x\\ge 1.$\n\nTherefore, the complete Cartesian equation is $y=({x}^{2}-1)(5-{x}^{2}),\\,x\\ge 1.$"}]}
